WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:07.373
This is a spirometer.
It's a tool to measure your
lung capacity and function.

00:00:07.406 --> 00:00:10.443
Medical tools like this one
are built with an adjustment.

00:00:10.476 --> 00:00:14.814
And it adjusts for race,
or as they call it in this
particular setting, origin.

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:18.217 line:0%
So, I'm gonna check
African-American

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:19.786
and then we'll try it again

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:22.522
and I'll change
my origin, in this case,

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:24.690
and we'll see what happens
when I blow into it.

00:00:24.723 --> 00:00:28.461
<i> Race adjustments,
 or race norms, are used
 all over medicine</i>

00:00:28.494 --> 00:00:29.796
<i> from assessing
 kidney function</i>

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:31.764
<i> to determining
 breast cancer risk</i>

00:00:31.797 --> 00:00:34.434
<i> and even diagnosing people
 with dementia.</i>

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:38.337
<i> The purpose is to make
 more accurate predictions
 about people's health.</i>

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.773
<i> But a recent lawsuit
 from retired NFL players</i>

00:00:40.806 --> 00:00:43.376
<i> accusing the practice
 of being discriminatory</i>

00:00:43.409 --> 00:00:45.778
<i> has brought national
 media attention</i>

00:00:45.811 --> 00:00:48.815
<i>to the potential harms of using
 race norming in medicine.</i>

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:51.584
Man: The practice,
widely known as "race norming,"

00:00:51.617 --> 00:00:54.587
makes it harder
for black players to qualify
for compensation.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.824
It establishes
a lower cognitive baseline

00:00:57.857 --> 00:00:59.692
for blacks than for whites.

00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:02.361 align:start size:84% position:16%
Basically you were done
before you started playing.

00:01:02.394 --> 00:01:06.566
Lee:<i> I need to learn more
 about why these adjustments
 are being used.</i>

00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:09.535
<i> Are there actual genetic
 differences between races?</i>

00:01:09.568 --> 00:01:12.305
<i> Where did the idea of race
 even come from?</i>

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:13.406
Let's see what it does.

00:01:22.648 --> 00:01:25.652 line:0%
<i> ( theme music playing )</i>

00:01:30.556 --> 00:01:31.891
So these are my results.

00:01:31.924 --> 00:01:34.627 line:0%
FEV stands for
forced expiratory volume.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.829 line:0%
It's the amount of breath
you can release in one second.

00:01:36.862 --> 00:01:40.800 line:0%
95%, FEV 3.6.

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:43.536
I'm a specimen
of athletic achievement.

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:47.507
So, compared to other black men
like me basically,

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:49.642
my lung functioning
rather is pretty healthy.

00:01:49.675 --> 00:01:52.445
So we're gonna do it
one more time,

00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:55.582
and this time we're gonna
change my origin to white.

00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:02.622 line:0%
So, um, I'm considered
mildly abnormal right now.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:06.459 line:0%
My numbers--
my FEV is 76 percentile.

00:02:06.492 --> 00:02:09.262 line:0%
If this was black,
it would have been
96 percentile.

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:12.265
I went from being very healthy
to lightly abnormal,

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:16.169
and I'm curious
of this dramatic adjustment
from white to black

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:18.938
could be the reason why
someone might be misdiagnosed

00:02:18.971 --> 00:02:20.306
just because
of their skin color.

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:25.611 line:0%
So you guys have heard
the saying that race
is a social construct,

00:02:25.644 --> 00:02:27.780
and I feel like
for the most part
we all believe that.

00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:31.184
But there's a 2018 survey
that said one in three Americans

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:34.587
actually believe there's
a biological difference
between the races,

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.456
and that's kind of what
I'm trying to investigate
right now.

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:40.226
So, I need you guys to do
one thing for me.

00:02:40.259 --> 00:02:42.962
Stand in front of this projector
and I'm gonna try and capture
your silhouette.

00:02:42.995 --> 00:02:44.197 align:start size:88% position:13%
Okay.

00:02:46.899 --> 00:02:48.434
Lee: Strike a pose.

00:02:49.835 --> 00:02:51.804 align:start size:66% position:34%
- Ooh.
- Amazing.

00:02:51.837 --> 00:02:53.573
Lee:
So you guys are both humans.

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:56.709
Everyone has between 20
and 25,000 genes,

00:02:56.742 --> 00:03:01.547
most of which, 99.9%,
are the same.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:05.785
It's that 0.1% that makes us
all individual or unique.

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:08.821
That 0.1% represents mutations
to your genetic code,

00:03:08.854 --> 00:03:11.958
which become genetic variants,
and those genetic variants

00:03:11.991 --> 00:03:14.360
can be anything
from physical things

00:03:14.393 --> 00:03:16.696
like skin color,
hair color, eye color.

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:20.433
But what I'm here to talk about
today is one very specific

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:22.768
genetic variant,
and that's skin color.

00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:26.539 align:start position:0%
All right,
so there are a few gene variants

00:03:26.572 --> 00:03:29.675 align:start position:0%
that are related to skin tone
and pigment, right?

00:03:29.708 --> 00:03:32.511 align:start position:0%
They're actually
what determine melanin
production in the body,

00:03:32.544 --> 00:03:35.514 align:start position:0%
and melanin is what
makes skin darker.

00:03:35.547 --> 00:03:37.750 align:start position:0%
And we know that there's
a correlation between

00:03:37.783 --> 00:03:40.553 align:start position:0%
exposure to the sun
and melanin.

00:03:42.721 --> 00:03:47.927 align:start position:0%
So dark skin helps protect
against the harmful effects
of ultraviolet light,

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:51.530 align:start position:0%
and the closer folks
were to the equator,
their skin remained darker.

00:03:51.563 --> 00:03:53.799 size:88% position:44%
And as we started to kind of
spread out across the globe,

00:03:53.832 --> 00:03:55.701 align:start size:66% position:34%
lighter skin evolved.

00:03:55.734 --> 00:03:57.003 align:start size:59% position:41%
Light skin helps
with the production

00:03:57.036 --> 00:03:58.604 align:start size:78% position:22%
of vitamin D in the body.

00:03:58.637 --> 00:04:00.539 align:start size:63% position:38%
At that latitudes,
they're less worried

00:04:00.572 --> 00:04:02.775
about getting too much sun
and more worried about--

00:04:02.808 --> 00:04:06.879
- Getting enough, right.
- Yeah, when you mentioned
  the survey of whether

00:04:06.912 --> 00:04:08.648 align:start size:59% position:41%
people think
race is biological,

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:11.317
I think basically this is
what they're thinking about.

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:13.519 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right.
- If they see people having
  different skin colors,

00:04:13.552 --> 00:04:15.788 align:start size:88% position:13%
some other different
features, and they're like,
"I can see a difference."

00:04:15.821 --> 00:04:19.592
And as you can see,
Africa has tons of diversity
and skin color.

00:04:19.625 --> 00:04:22.428 align:start size:84% position:16%
Yeah, so when people try to
define a race category,

00:04:22.461 --> 00:04:24.497 align:start size:56% position:44%
clearly they can't
use skin color.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.332 align:start position:0%
It's kind of laughable,
honestly.

00:04:26.365 --> 00:04:28.901
So, like, I mean,
this map captures skin color,

00:04:28.934 --> 00:04:33.472
but it does not account
for other physical mutations
that happened over time.

00:04:33.505 --> 00:04:36.075 align:start size:69% position:31%
I think when you would
look at those as well,

00:04:36.108 --> 00:04:41.314
you would find that
they don't map onto these
continents so clearly.

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:44.450
I think that if you try to find
physical characteristics

00:04:44.483 --> 00:04:47.086
of the racial categories,
you're going to fail every time.

00:04:47.119 --> 00:04:50.923 align:start position:0%
In the last 500 years,
there's been even more movement

00:04:50.956 --> 00:04:53.526 size:84% position:42%
of humans across the globe,

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:57.997 align:start position:0%
and a lot of that was due
to the forced migration--
the transatlantic slave trade.

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:02.001 align:start position:0%
And with this increased exposure
and mixture of skin colors,

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:03.969 align:start position:0%
inferences were starting
to be made

00:05:04.002 --> 00:05:08.007 align:start position:0%
about what someone's
skin color meant about them.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.511 align:start position:0%
The idea that there was
a difference between races
and skin tones,

00:05:12.544 --> 00:05:14.547
people started to succumb to
some pretty wild conclusions.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.750 align:start position:0%
I want to show you guys
what I mean when I say that.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:26.392
So this is John Brown.
A man named Dr. Hamilton was
performing tests,

00:05:26.425 --> 00:05:29.462
<i> experiments on John's skin,
 to prove that black people</i>

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:31.630
<i> had thicker skin
 than white people.</i>

00:05:31.663 --> 00:05:33.466
<i> Their skin was better able</i>

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:35.634
<i> to tolerate working
 outside in the sun,</i>

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:38.904
<i> and he would do things
 like expose John's skin</i>

00:05:38.937 --> 00:05:42.575
<i> to extremely dangerous levels
 of direct sunlight.</i>

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:45.945
And he did this for years
to try and justify

00:05:45.978 --> 00:05:49.849
the belief that blacks were
meant to be slaves biologically.

00:05:49.882 --> 00:05:55.054 align:start position:0%
But this belief of
skin thickness, it lingered
through medicine to today,

00:05:55.087 --> 00:05:58.124
and it's being taught to kids
in medical school right now.

00:05:58.157 --> 00:05:59.992
So, I'm a second year
medical student right now.

00:06:00.025 --> 00:06:01.560
My roommate reminded me
of a time

00:06:01.593 --> 00:06:02.995 line:0%
when he was
in a suture clinic

00:06:03.028 --> 00:06:05.498 line:0%
practicing his
suturing skills on a banana.

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:08.834 line:0%
He asked the question of,
"How do you know what
needle size to use

00:06:08.867 --> 00:06:10.636 line:0%
and how do you know
what thread size to use?"

00:06:10.669 --> 00:06:13.539
The physician said it correlates
with the thickness of the skin.

00:06:13.572 --> 00:06:16.008 line:0%
So for example, the skin
in your eyelid is much thinner

00:06:16.041 --> 00:06:17.676 line:0%
than the skin
in your lower back.

00:06:17.709 --> 00:06:19.111
But then they said
that needle size can also

00:06:19.144 --> 00:06:22.114 line:0%
correlate with
a person's ethnicity,

00:06:22.147 --> 00:06:24.850
implying that different
skin colors have different

00:06:24.883 --> 00:06:27.186
thickness of skin,
which isn't true.

00:06:27.219 --> 00:06:28.721 line:0%
That's literally how racism

00:06:28.754 --> 00:06:30.656 line:0%
is perpetuated in
the field of medicine.

00:06:30.689 --> 00:06:33.859 align:start size:91% position:9%
I would imagine
we see similar misconceptions

00:06:33.892 --> 00:06:35.761 align:start size:69% position:31%
around pain tolerance,
too, right?

00:06:35.794 --> 00:06:38.631 align:start size:69% position:31%
I feel like
that goes hand in hand
with skin thickness.

00:06:38.664 --> 00:06:41.867
The belief that one skin color
or one person of one race

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.703
is superior to another,
that is racism.

00:06:44.736 --> 00:06:46.806
But that's not really what
I'm trying to investigate.

00:06:46.839 --> 00:06:48.174
What I'm trying to investigate

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:50.910
is what role race
plays in medicine.

00:06:50.943 --> 00:06:53.579 align:start position:0%
It seems like
a really tough question

00:06:53.612 --> 00:06:57.183 align:start position:0%
because you're
talking about going,
like, more than skin deep

00:06:57.216 --> 00:07:00.619 align:start position:0%
in asking whether there
are biological differences

00:07:00.652 --> 00:07:02.555 align:start position:0%
in the way
that our health develops

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:05.024
and the kind of conditions
that we might be at risk for.

00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:06.725
I'm a carrier of
the sickle cell anemia trait.

00:07:06.758 --> 00:07:08.060 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Oh, right.
- For years, we thought

00:07:08.093 --> 00:07:09.195
that was
a black disease, right?

00:07:09.228 --> 00:07:11.197 align:start position:0%
That's what people
think of it, yeah.

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:15.768 align:start position:0%
Because it's so prevalent
among this one kind
of group of the world

00:07:15.801 --> 00:07:17.169 align:start position:0%
and less prevalent
everywhere else,

00:07:17.202 --> 00:07:20.206 align:start position:0%
we label that with
a kind of racial category.

00:07:20.239 --> 00:07:23.909
Yeah, this idea that there
are diseases, right,

00:07:23.942 --> 00:07:28.514
or medical issues tied
to a specific race, it's wild.

00:07:30.215 --> 00:07:35.921 align:start position:0% line:0%
When we go to the doctor
and we check a box for race,

00:07:35.954 --> 00:07:38.524 size:75% position:38%
what is that information
actually being used for?

00:07:38.557 --> 00:07:43.996 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
You being compared
to an average distribution
of healthy individuals

00:07:44.029 --> 00:07:47.733 align:start size:69% position:31% line:0%
of your sex, your age,
and your race.

00:07:47.766 --> 00:07:51.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Why is it being utilized?
- We know that blood
  characteristics

00:07:51.703 --> 00:07:57.676 align:start size:81% position:19%
like your red blood cells,
your white blood cells,
differ by race.

00:07:57.709 --> 00:08:01.547 size:84% position:58%
And so when I'm telling you
your white blood cell count

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:05.818 align:start size:75% position:25%
is X number, 100,
what does that 100 mean?

00:08:05.851 --> 00:08:09.655 align:start size:75% position:25%
It only means something
if you're being compared
to a population average.

00:08:09.688 --> 00:08:12.925 align:start size:59% position:41%
So I had a patient,
a firefighter,

00:08:12.958 --> 00:08:15.961 align:start size:69% position:31%
and when we were doing
the test on his lungs,

00:08:15.994 --> 00:08:20.099 align:start size:75% position:25%
the technician
immediately compared him
to African-Americans.

00:08:20.132 --> 00:08:24.136 size:84% position:58%
But I asked him what he was
and he said, "I'm half."

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:25.971 align:start size:50% position:50%
And in his case,

00:08:26.004 --> 00:08:30.576 align:start size:59% position:41%
it benefitted him 
to be labeled white

00:08:30.609 --> 00:08:32.645 align:start size:75% position:25%
so he qualified
for disability benefits.

00:08:32.678 --> 00:08:36.982 align:start position:0%
How much do
social determinants
affect health outcomes?

00:08:37.015 --> 00:08:40.252 align:start position:0%
Things like housing,
things like exposure
to air pollution,

00:08:40.285 --> 00:08:42.821 align:start position:0%
socioeconomic status,
all those things.

00:08:42.854 --> 00:08:47.192 align:start size:75% position:25%
I think your zip code
for non-biologic reasons
is a bigger predictor

00:08:47.225 --> 00:08:50.663 align:start size:66% position:34%
of health
and health inequities
than anything.

00:08:50.696 --> 00:08:53.966 size:88% position:44%
Shouldn't those factors then
be included in conjunction

00:08:53.999 --> 00:08:55.968 align:start position:0%
with things like race
and age and gender?

00:08:56.001 --> 00:08:58.637 align:start size:69% position:31%
So that's what we call
an empiric question.

00:08:58.670 --> 00:09:00.639
We don't know the answer yet.

00:09:00.672 --> 00:09:04.677 align:start size:78% position:22%
Until we do the studies,
we can't definitively say

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:10.849 align:start size:81% position:19%
if X disease is due
to social environmental
factors or purely biology.

00:09:10.882 --> 00:09:15.220
Do you think these-- that this
race stratification in medicine

00:09:15.253 --> 00:09:18.290 align:start position:0%
could potentially overstate
how much of a biological
difference there is

00:09:18.323 --> 00:09:21.126 align:start position:0%
between members
of different races?

00:09:21.159 --> 00:09:24.630 align:start size:84% position:16%
We could be either
overestimating your disease

00:09:24.663 --> 00:09:26.932 align:start size:56% position:44%
or underestimating
your disease,

00:09:26.965 --> 00:09:29.702 align:start size:63% position:38%
and I can't give you
a blanket statement.

00:09:29.735 --> 00:09:33.105 align:end position:100%
We need to do the work
to actually determine

00:09:33.138 --> 00:09:38.044 align:start size:88% position:13%
what is the normal
population distribution
for different racial groups.

00:09:40.278 --> 00:09:42.881
So it makes sense that
you would want to be compared

00:09:42.914 --> 00:09:45.384
to a population
that's similar to you,

00:09:45.417 --> 00:09:47.886
but should skin color
be a part of that?

00:09:47.919 --> 00:09:51.123
I've been looking online
for studies or situations

00:09:51.156 --> 00:09:54.393
where race norming
has caused harm,

00:09:54.426 --> 00:09:57.896
but if you were misdiagnosed
because of this,

00:09:57.929 --> 00:09:59.732
it would be hard to quantify,

00:09:59.765 --> 00:10:02.234
mostly because patients
wouldn't be aware

00:10:02.267 --> 00:10:04.803
that these adjustments
were being used.

00:10:04.836 --> 00:10:09.341
It's more about
the potential harm
if doctors start to rely

00:10:09.374 --> 00:10:13.312
on metrics that have
no biological basis,

00:10:13.345 --> 00:10:16.382
and it seems like it's an area
that's only beginning
to be studied.

00:10:16.415 --> 00:10:20.319
I have found one recent case
of the consequences
of race norming.

00:10:20.352 --> 00:10:22.355
It actually concerns the NFL.

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:34.933
So the race adjustments were
used in the neurological
assessments

00:10:34.966 --> 00:10:37.036
of these former NFL athletes.

00:10:37.069 --> 00:10:39.271
I'm getting ready to meet
Kevin Henry.

00:10:39.304 --> 00:10:42.408
<i> He spent eight seasons playing
 defensive end in the NFL,</i>

00:10:42.441 --> 00:10:45.277
and he took out a lawsuit
against the NFL

00:10:45.310 --> 00:10:47.313
specifically because
of this race norming.

00:10:51.283 --> 00:10:55.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
- See them divots in there?
- I see that. There's a lot
  of scratches in it.

00:10:55.821 --> 00:10:58.924 align:start position:0%
What does eight seasons
in the NFL do to someone

00:10:58.957 --> 00:11:01.860 align:start position:0%
physically, mentally,
emotionally?

00:11:01.893 --> 00:11:05.798 align:start size:66% position:34% line:0%
My eight year career
was full of injuries,

00:11:05.831 --> 00:11:08.834 align:start size:63% position:38% line:0%
full of things I had
to fight through.

00:11:08.867 --> 00:11:10.235
<i> Both ankles,
 one of them twice.</i>

00:11:10.268 --> 00:11:14.139
<i> Both knees, both elbows,
 both wrists.</i>

00:11:14.172 --> 00:11:16.108
<i> All my fingers been broke
 at least once.</i>

00:11:16.141 --> 00:11:20.079 align:start size:63% position:38%
Both shoulders.
I've had ten or more
concussions,

00:11:20.112 --> 00:11:21.714 align:start size:84% position:16%
and those are just the ones
that are documented.

00:11:21.747 --> 00:11:23.282 align:start position:0%
I've talked to a couple
of other guys who said

00:11:23.315 --> 00:11:25.451 align:start position:0%
they would be kind of
in a fog normally.

00:11:25.484 --> 00:11:31.190 align:start size:94% position:6%
Right, right.
It takes you just a little bit
longer to make decisions.

00:11:31.223 --> 00:11:36.061 align:start position:0%
What was it like watching
Kevin go through this
for eight seasons?

00:11:36.094 --> 00:11:40.232 align:start position:0% line:0%
It was horrible.
He sits there-- "This is
hurting, this is hurting.

00:11:40.265 --> 00:11:42.735 align:start position:0% line:0%
My head is hurting."
Like, the memories--

00:11:42.768 --> 00:11:47.473 align:start position:0%
I'm the person that has
to make sure, you know,

00:11:47.506 --> 00:11:50.309
the doors are locked,
the oven's turned off.

00:11:50.342 --> 00:11:54.279 align:start size:66% position:34%
I don't care how much
money you have,

00:11:54.312 --> 00:11:56.882
what kind of shape you're in,

00:11:56.915 --> 00:11:59.451 align:start size:75% position:25%
you can't do anything
without that brain, man.

00:11:59.484 --> 00:12:03.388 align:start position:0%
Initially,
what was your diagnosis
and what did the NFL say?

00:12:03.421 --> 00:12:06.125 align:start size:81% position:19%
They denied me altogether.

00:12:06.158 --> 00:12:10.162 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then I kept going back,
you know, getting reevaluated.

00:12:10.195 --> 00:12:12.798 align:start position:0%
His lawyer did the research
and he was like,

00:12:12.831 --> 00:12:15.033 align:start size:88% position:13%
- something's not right.
- From what I read,

00:12:15.066 --> 00:12:20.339 align:start position:0%
it seemed like they were saying
that you entered the league

00:12:20.372 --> 00:12:23.809 align:start position:0%
at a lower level of
cognitive functioning
than your white counterparts.

00:12:23.842 --> 00:12:26.145 align:start position:0%
Which meant
that you needed to show

00:12:26.178 --> 00:12:28.981 align:start position:0%
even more severe
cognitive decline.

00:12:29.014 --> 00:12:32.017 align:start size:88% position:13%
Basically, "You were dumb
before you started playing."

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:36.088 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Oh, my God.
- "So, if you're claiming
  that you're impaired now--"

00:12:36.121 --> 00:12:38.390
He was already impaired.

00:12:38.423 --> 00:12:42.060
Lee:<i> Kevin was recently
 reevaluated and diagnosed
 with mild impairment,</i>

00:12:42.093 --> 00:12:45.864
<i> which can lead to some
 treatment and monitoring,
 but no money.</i>

00:12:45.897 --> 00:12:48.233
<i> His lawyer claims
 that without race norming,</i>

00:12:48.266 --> 00:12:50.969
<i> his scores would have shown
 more severe impairment</i>

00:12:51.002 --> 00:12:54.139
<i> and could have qualified him
 for a monetary reward.</i>

00:12:54.172 --> 00:12:57.209 align:start position:0%
What drove you to file
the lawsuit initially?

00:12:57.242 --> 00:13:01.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
I wanted this whole
race norming thing eradicated.

00:13:01.112 --> 00:13:07.052 align:start size:72% position:28%
It allows them to have
an escape hatch, to me,
for the payments.

00:13:07.085 --> 00:13:09.888 align:start position:0%
What would that--
that compensation
mean to your life?

00:13:09.921 --> 00:13:15.294
So I'm in a situation right now
where who's going to hire me?

00:13:15.327 --> 00:13:17.296 align:start size:59% position:41%
I've been diagnosed
with dementia.

00:13:17.329 --> 00:13:20.399 align:start size:75% position:25%
How can I find a job?
I still have a mortgage.

00:13:20.432 --> 00:13:24.603
You know, I still got a family
to take care of, to support.

00:13:24.636 --> 00:13:28.473 size:88% position:56%
I don't like talking about--

00:13:28.506 --> 00:13:30.442 align:start size:63% position:38%
I don't like talking
about suicide.

00:13:30.475 --> 00:13:33.579 align:start size:78% position:22%
But everything
that I got going on, man,

00:13:33.612 --> 00:13:36.048 align:start size:75% position:25%
I'm battling depression.

00:13:36.081 --> 00:13:40.853 align:start position:0%
Like, I just see this man
deteriorating every single day,
and it just breaks my heart.

00:13:40.886 --> 00:13:44.523 align:start position:0%
It extends beyond just,
"I need help to pay
my mortgage."

00:13:44.556 --> 00:13:46.992 align:start size:91% position:9%
- You need help to manage
  your life.
- Right.

00:13:47.025 --> 00:13:51.163 align:start size:75% position:25%
At least give us
some tools to work with.

00:13:51.196 --> 00:13:53.232
Lee:<i> The judge dismissed
 Kevin's lawsuit,</i>

00:13:53.265 --> 00:13:56.101
<i> but ordered the NFL and
the original settlement lawyers</i>

00:13:56.134 --> 00:13:57.636
<i> to address
 the race norming issue.</i>

00:13:57.669 --> 00:14:00.305
<i> An NFL spokesperson
 told me,</i>

00:14:00.338 --> 00:14:02.975
<i> "The availability
 of demographic adjustments</i>

00:14:03.008 --> 00:14:05.310
<i> was designed
 to avoid the misdiagnosis</i>

00:14:05.343 --> 00:14:08.480
<i> of healthy individuals
 as cognitively impaired.</i>

00:14:08.513 --> 00:14:10.616
<i> The NFL nevertheless
 is committed to helping</i>

00:14:10.649 --> 00:14:13.151
<i> find alternative
 testing techniques</i>

00:14:13.184 --> 00:14:15.487
<i> that will lead
 to diagnostic accuracy</i>

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:18.290
<i> without employing
 race-based norms."</i>

00:14:30.135 --> 00:14:33.005
The thing is,
the more I hear about this,

00:14:33.038 --> 00:14:36.475
the more I feel like race
is not a reliable category

00:14:36.508 --> 00:14:39.044
for measuring
health differences.

00:14:39.077 --> 00:14:43.883
And I keep thinking about
something that Dr. Burchard
told me when we last spoke.

00:14:45.517 --> 00:14:48.487
Burchard: Race and ethnicity
are social constructs.

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:50.555
They're artificial
social constructs,

00:14:50.588 --> 00:14:54.660
and it's different than
genetic ancestry.

00:14:54.693 --> 00:14:57.462
So genetic ancestry
matters more than race?

00:14:57.495 --> 00:15:00.232
I'm gonna go learn
a bit more about mine.

00:15:02.667 --> 00:15:05.671 align:start position:0%
We talked about how race
can be a pretty crude metric
for skin tone.

00:15:05.704 --> 00:15:10.542 align:start position:0%
The thing is,
race does correlate with
certain genetic traits.

00:15:10.575 --> 00:15:12.544 align:start position:0%
I carry the sickle cell
anemia trait.

00:15:12.577 --> 00:15:15.347 align:start size:81% position:19%
It turns out the trait
is a mutation that evolved

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:19.518 align:start size:84% position:16%
in regions of the world
where malaria was a major
problem and cause of death.

00:15:19.551 --> 00:15:21.687 align:start position:0%
People who carry
the sickle cell anemia trait,

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:24.056 align:start size:56% position:44%
you're actually
somewhat immunized

00:15:24.089 --> 00:15:27.259 align:start size:81% position:19%
from the most debilitating
parts of malaria.

00:15:27.292 --> 00:15:29.294
So this is what we call
a selective pressure.

00:15:29.327 --> 00:15:35.233 align:start size:75% position:25%
And that means that
I have genetic ancestry
in sub-Saharan Africa,

00:15:35.266 --> 00:15:37.169 align:start size:69% position:31%
where malaria is
just really prevalent.

00:15:37.202 --> 00:15:39.237 size:84% position:42%
We took some 23andMe tests,

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:41.206 align:start position:0%
so I am curious what
my results are gonna be.

00:15:41.239 --> 00:15:43.575
- And you really don't know?
- What I'm assuming then is,

00:15:43.608 --> 00:15:46.445 size:84% position:58%
like, 80% African ancestry.

00:15:46.478 --> 00:15:51.016 align:start size:78% position:22%
And when you look at,
you know, the possibility
of doing genetic testing,

00:15:51.049 --> 00:15:55.721
it can't actually tell you
anything about exactly where
your family members are from.

00:15:55.754 --> 00:16:00.492 align:start size:78% position:22%
It can just tell you
what pools of DNA
your DNA correlates with.

00:16:00.525 --> 00:16:02.995 align:start size:69% position:31%
- You ready?
- Yeah.

00:16:05.063 --> 00:16:06.631 align:start position:0%
What the ( bleep )?

00:16:06.664 --> 00:16:09.568
This isn't mine.
This is Christophe's.

00:16:13.138 --> 00:16:17.109 align:start position:0% line:0%
What? What?
I'm gonna call my mom.

00:16:17.142 --> 00:16:19.645
( line ringing )

00:16:22.647 --> 00:16:24.082 line:0%
Mom:
<i> Hello, my love.</i>

00:16:24.115 --> 00:16:28.620
Uh, I'm looking
at my 23andMe results.

00:16:28.653 --> 00:16:31.056
Why am I half white?

00:16:31.089 --> 00:16:32.557
I don't even know
who I am anymore.

00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:37.062
40%? It only says
53% sub-Saharan African.

00:16:37.095 --> 00:16:39.431
Do you know--
that's barely half.

00:16:39.464 --> 00:16:41.366 line:0%
Where did all this white
come from?

00:16:41.399 --> 00:16:44.636 line:0%
<i> My mother.
 And probably from Daddy, too.</i>

00:16:44.669 --> 00:16:46.438 align:start size:47% position:53%
Mom, the math's
not adding up.

00:16:46.471 --> 00:16:48.473 align:start size:72% position:28%
All right, I love you.
I'll talk to you later.

00:16:48.506 --> 00:16:50.475 line:0%
<i> I love you,
 too, sweetie. Bye.</i>

00:16:50.508 --> 00:16:52.511 line:0%
Okay, bye.

00:16:52.544 --> 00:16:54.646 size:81% position:41%
But, Lee, you just told us
that there's a difference

00:16:54.679 --> 00:16:57.315 align:start position:0%
between race
and genetic ancestries.

00:16:57.348 --> 00:16:59.718 align:start size:91% position:9%
- So you're not 40% white.
- White.

00:16:59.751 --> 00:17:04.122 align:start position:0%
- You share 40% of your DNA
  with people who are European.
- With white people.

00:17:04.155 --> 00:17:09.795 align:start position:0%
But it gets to the point.
Just because your DNA shows
a bunch of European,

00:17:09.828 --> 00:17:11.563 align:start position:0%
it doesn't change the way
that society is gonna treat you.

00:17:11.596 --> 00:17:15.734
Views me. Right, exactly.
I don't even know if I--

00:17:15.767 --> 00:17:17.803
Am I mixed-race?
Like, is that--

00:17:17.836 --> 00:17:20.539 align:start position:0%
And if you are,
what proportion of
African-Americans are?

00:17:20.572 --> 00:17:24.276 align:start size:75% position:25%
Are mixed-race?
Probably a good majority
of us, right?

00:17:24.309 --> 00:17:27.312
Which kind of makes
race so comical.

00:17:27.345 --> 00:17:30.482
Because obviously my genetic
and thus medical picture

00:17:30.515 --> 00:17:35.187
should be way more nuanced than
a black reference population.

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:39.591
The ideal would be
for everyone to have some kind
of profile like this,

00:17:39.624 --> 00:17:42.594 align:start size:69% position:31%
and then that is your
more accurate picture.

00:17:42.627 --> 00:17:43.829
What you were just describing

00:17:43.862 --> 00:17:45.831
is kind of the future
of medicine

00:17:45.864 --> 00:17:49.167
that geneticists are
working toward right now.

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:53.805
I'm gonna go talk
to this geneticist
who works at Columbia.

00:17:53.838 --> 00:17:56.208
He's writing a research program
called the All Of Us Program,

00:17:56.241 --> 00:17:59.144
and they want to genetically
sequence a million people

00:17:59.177 --> 00:18:01.213
from all over the United States

00:18:01.246 --> 00:18:03.782
so that we can move closer
to precision medicine.

00:18:10.588 --> 00:18:13.658 size:84% position:42% line:0%
I know over the last decade
we've seen these, you know,

00:18:13.691 --> 00:18:18.563 align:start position:0%
tremendous advancements
in the way we understand
genetic ancestry.

00:18:18.596 --> 00:18:22.367 align:start position:0%
Is that more useful
to medical professionals

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:24.136 align:start position:0% line:0%
or to the field of medicine
than race?

00:18:24.169 --> 00:18:26.872 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
When we're thinking about
genetics in the clinic,

00:18:26.905 --> 00:18:28.673 align:start size:63% position:38% line:0%
we don't care
where they're from.

00:18:28.706 --> 00:18:31.576 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
We care what's sitting in
and that individual's genome,

00:18:31.609 --> 00:18:35.280 align:start size:78% position:22%
not where their ancestors
came from geographically.

00:18:35.313 --> 00:18:38.583 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think the best way
to think of race in medicine

00:18:38.616 --> 00:18:42.254 align:start size:53% position:47%
is as a proxy
for other things,

00:18:42.287 --> 00:18:45.590 align:start size:81% position:19%
and a lousy proxy at that.

00:18:45.623 --> 00:18:49.895
We came across this story about
hepatitis C and how, you know,

00:18:49.928 --> 00:18:51.763
there were different outcomes

00:18:51.796 --> 00:18:53.932 align:start position:0%
in white patients
versus black patients.

00:18:53.965 --> 00:18:58.904
<i> 70 million people worldwide
 are infected with hepatitis C,</i>

00:18:58.937 --> 00:19:01.740
<i> a blood-borne virus
 that causes liver infection.</i>

00:19:01.773 --> 00:19:03.308
<i> Practitioners were seeing</i>

00:19:03.341 --> 00:19:05.443
<i> that patients
 of European ancestry</i>

00:19:05.476 --> 00:19:07.779
<i> recovered more easily
 from infection</i>

00:19:07.812 --> 00:19:10.248
<i> than patients
 of African ancestry.</i>

00:19:10.281 --> 00:19:12.617
<i> However,
 a study found that DNA,</i>

00:19:12.650 --> 00:19:16.621
<i> not geographic ancestry,
 better explained why.</i>

00:19:16.654 --> 00:19:19.524 align:start size:59% position:41%
One particular part
of the genome had
a dramatic impact

00:19:19.557 --> 00:19:21.726 align:start size:50% position:50%
on who was cured
and who wasn't.

00:19:21.759 --> 00:19:25.664 align:start size:78% position:22%
Some people have the good
response gene variant,

00:19:25.697 --> 00:19:28.400 align:start size:94% position:6%
and some people have
the bad response gene variant.

00:19:28.433 --> 00:19:32.204
Lee:<i> It was thought that
 black patients didn't respond
 well to the treatment.</i>

00:19:32.237 --> 00:19:34.673
<i> But upon further examination
 of the genome,</i>

00:19:34.706 --> 00:19:38.376
<i> it was discovered that
 23% to 55% of black patients</i>

00:19:38.409 --> 00:19:41.980
<i> did in fact carry
 the good response
 gene variants,</i>

00:19:42.013 --> 00:19:44.649
<i> meaning they would
 have responded well
 to the treatment.</i>

00:19:44.682 --> 00:19:47.319
<i> But because physicians were
 making treatment decisions</i>

00:19:47.352 --> 00:19:50.655
<i> based on correlations
 with geographic ancestry</i>

00:19:50.688 --> 00:19:54.593
<i> instead of looking
 at each individual
 patient's gene variants,</i>

00:19:54.626 --> 00:19:57.829
<i> black patients were
 missing out on treatment.</i>

00:19:57.862 --> 00:20:00.398 align:start position:0%
The reason why race
was a bad proxy was because

00:20:00.431 --> 00:20:03.969 size:84% position:42%
it's not impossible to have
the gene as a black person.

00:20:04.002 --> 00:20:07.906 align:start position:0%
It's just less likely than,
you know, your white
or your Asian counterparts.

00:20:07.939 --> 00:20:10.842 align:start size:75% position:25%
And so what it tells you
is that you want to
look at the individual.

00:20:10.875 --> 00:20:14.012 align:start position:0%
I mean,
this explains how folks
could be misdiagnosed

00:20:14.045 --> 00:20:16.648 align:start position:0%
or just miss out in this case
on treatment entirely

00:20:16.681 --> 00:20:19.684 align:start position:0%
because we used race as a proxy,
and we just thought,

00:20:19.717 --> 00:20:22.520 align:start position:0%
"Oh, no.
Black folks aren't responding
well to this treatment."

00:20:22.553 --> 00:20:24.956
We don't want to be making
important treatment decisions

00:20:24.989 --> 00:20:27.525 align:start size:81% position:19%
based on weak correlations

00:20:27.558 --> 00:20:30.962
with geographic ancestry or race

00:20:30.995 --> 00:20:32.931 align:start size:81% position:19%
when we can do way better.

00:20:32.964 --> 00:20:35.333 align:start size:56% position:44%
There are not even
that many examples
in the first place

00:20:35.366 --> 00:20:37.869 size:81% position:59%
where geographic ancestry
really makes a difference.

00:20:37.902 --> 00:20:41.406 align:start size:91% position:9%
And the reason is that
we're all darn near the same.

00:20:41.439 --> 00:20:45.343 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's of course
a few little places in
the genome where we differ.

00:20:45.376 --> 00:20:48.546 align:start size:72% position:28%
Sometimes the reasons
are because there have
been different patterns

00:20:48.579 --> 00:20:51.383 align:start size:59% position:41%
of exposure to
infectious diseases

00:20:51.416 --> 00:20:52.717 align:start size:56% position:44%
in different parts
of the world.

00:20:52.750 --> 00:20:55.687
Both:
Like sickle cell and malaria.

00:20:55.720 --> 00:20:58.323 align:start position:0%
Can we get rid
of race in medicine?

00:20:58.356 --> 00:21:02.994 align:start size:75% position:25%
I do have sympathy
for the perspective that
we should consider that.

00:21:03.027 --> 00:21:07.332 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't think that it
would be the best way to
practice medicine right now.

00:21:07.365 --> 00:21:10.335 align:start size:63% position:38%
The best way
to practice medicine

00:21:10.368 --> 00:21:12.737 align:start size:66% position:34%
is to not consider it
unless you need to.

00:21:12.770 --> 00:21:16.041 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And I will keep going back
  to the hepatitis C example.
- Love that example.

00:21:16.074 --> 00:21:19.344 size:84% position:58%
Let's say that we weren't
permitted to consider race.

00:21:19.377 --> 00:21:22.047 align:start size:75% position:25%
The observation then
would not have been made
that individuals

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:24.616 align:start size:66% position:34%
of different
geographic ancestries
respond differently.

00:21:29.387 --> 00:21:32.057
Race may have come from
thinking about skin color,

00:21:32.090 --> 00:21:34.926
but the implications mean
so much more than that.

00:21:34.959 --> 00:21:37.362
My skin color determines
how I'm perceived,

00:21:37.395 --> 00:21:39.531
and that in turn
affects my health.

00:21:39.564 --> 00:21:42.400
So from day one, it's been
really difficult for me

00:21:42.433 --> 00:21:45.403
to wrap my head around
this question

00:21:45.436 --> 00:21:49.774
because it seems like using
a metric as vague as race

00:21:49.807 --> 00:21:52.777
<i> can carry with it a lot
 of incorrect assumptions.</i>

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:54.779
<i> However, there
 are some correlations</i>

00:21:54.812 --> 00:21:58.917
<i> with genetic ancestry
 that seem to be helpful.</i>

00:21:58.950 --> 00:22:01.820
So, I've realized that
we need to support efforts

00:22:01.853 --> 00:22:04.456
that will allow us
to remove the use of race

00:22:04.489 --> 00:22:07.559
as a proxy for how we
administer treatment to folks

00:22:07.592 --> 00:22:09.961
<i> or how we determine
 if someone's healthy.</i>

00:22:09.994 --> 00:22:13.031
<i> In response
 to a movement largely led
 by medical students,</i>

00:22:13.064 --> 00:22:14.799
<i> some hospitals
 and universities</i>

00:22:14.832 --> 00:22:16.968
<i> are removing certain
 race adjustments.</i>

00:22:17.001 --> 00:22:19.871
We need to find solutions
that fix inequities

00:22:19.904 --> 00:22:23.575
and not use race
as a best guess.

00:22:25.143 --> 00:22:27.479
A few weeks after
we wrapped this episode,

00:22:27.512 --> 00:22:30.115
the NFL pledged to stop
using race norming

00:22:30.148 --> 00:22:33.651
as a part of how they were
evaluating brain injury claims,

00:22:33.684 --> 00:22:35.420
meaning that players
like Kevin Henry

00:22:35.453 --> 00:22:38.490
will likely be reevaluated
under a new testing protocol

00:22:38.523 --> 00:22:40.492
that won't involve race.

00:22:40.525 --> 00:22:42.660
It's still unclear
what that method will be

00:22:42.693 --> 00:22:46.031
and what effect it will have
on future brain injury claims.

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:52.504 align:start position:0% line:0%
Uh, this is Best Episode,
act one, take one.

00:22:52.537 --> 00:22:54.672 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
- Take two, marker.
- Did you say--

00:22:54.705 --> 00:22:56.941 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
- Best episode?
- Damn.

00:22:56.974 --> 00:22:59.778 line:0%
Man: Careful.

00:22:59.811 --> 00:23:02.147 line:0%
This is mildly abnormal.

00:23:02.180 --> 00:23:03.982 line:0%
I need to call a doctor.

